  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMidday water vapor satellite imagery clearly defined a strong\nclosed cyclone over SW Arizona. Midlevel moisture continued to\nstream northward into SW KS, with areas of virga, sprinkles or\nlight rain noted on radar across the western counties. The\nsynoptic regime is stationary, or is only crawling eastward \ntoday and tonight, as such forcing for ascent and any rainfall \nwill make minimal (if any) eastward progress into Kansas through\ntonight. Midlevel clouds will thicken this afternoon, but \ndespite this, mild southerly breezes will keep temperatures well\nabove late October normals this afternoon. Surface observations\nat noon depicted a strong cold front over northeast Colorado \nand northwest Kansas. This boundary will make some progress \nsouthward into SW KS through tonight with a northeast wind \nshift, before weakening and washing out through sunrise Sunday.\n\nNumerous showers and thunderstorms over eastern New Mexico and\nsoutheast Colorado Saturday afternoon are forecast by models to\nremain essentially in place through 7 am Sunday, with synoptic\neastward translation at a snail's pace. As such, any pops for\nrain showers are limited to areas west of US 83 through 7 am\nSunday. Most of SW KS will remain dry tonight and unseasonably\nmild in the 50s. \n\nPrimary trough axis will make minimal eastward progress Sunday,\naligning near the Four Corners by 7 pm. A rich midlevel moisture\nplume will continue on the eastern periphery of the cyclone's\ncirculation, with again minimal eastward progress, thanks to a\nblocking upstream midlevel ridge axis over the Mississippi\nvalley. Widespread rain in this moist conveyor belt will make\nsome eastward progress Sunday, but at a painstakingly slow pace,\nperhaps to US 283 by early afternoon. Clearly, highest pops and\nQPF grids are correctly placed west of US 83 through midday.\nVarious CAMs and 12z NAM suggest locally heavy rain banding is \nprobable along/west of US 83 by 4 pm Sunday, with little if any \nrain Dodge City eastward. Instability and lapse rates will be \nquite poor and limited, with no severe weather expected, and \nany thunder very limited. South to north training of rainfall \nbands, with little eastward translation, may increase flooding \nor hydrology issues at least locally Sunday afternoon. Very \nclear there will be a very sharp gradient in rainfall totals \nfrom this event - much more west, much less east. \n\nFinally Sunday night through Monday, the parent trough and\nembedded shortwaves will eject into western Kansas, with\nimproving forcing for ascent and shower activity more\nefficiently spreading eastward. NBM pops remain in the scattered\ncategory through Monday night until the trough passes SW KS\ncompletely, and subsidence returns dry weather by Tuesday. \n\nA pair of beautiful fall days are forecast Wednesday and\nThursday, with both afternoon and sunrise temperatures well\nabove late October normals. 70s will be common both afternoons,\nwith some locations reaching 80 degrees at peak heating each\nday. Strong south winds are expected Friday, establishing strong\nmoisture return and dryline maintenance, with scattered\nthunderstorms returning Friday afternoon/evening. \n\nNo cold air intrusions are progged by model guidance through \nthe next 10 days, with confidence SW KS will see no freezes for\nthe remainder of October.",
